 "about": "A way into this dataset from an ordinary chapter-and-verse reference. The eighty-six literary units are a division proposed by this research; they are not a division the wider world already uses, and they do not follow the chapter breaks. This file lets anyone holding a normal reference — Genesis 15:6, Leviticus 19 — find the unit that contains it without knowing the division in advance.",
 "how_to_use": "For a whole chapter, read by_chapter: it gives the unit or units covering that chapter. For a single verse, scan that book’s ranges for the entry whose start is at or before your reference and whose end is at or after it. Every verse of the Torah lies in exactly one unit, so the scan always succeeds and never returns two answers.",
 "coverage": {
  "verses_in_the_torah": 5846,
  "verses_placed_in_a_unit": 5846,
  "units_run_continuously": true,
  "units_overlap": false,
  "verse_counts_checked_against": "Sefaria chapter lengths",
  "unreadable_verse_numbers": [
   "Genesis 21:2",
   "Exodus 1:3",
   "Numbers 3:10"
  ],
  "unreadable_note": "Three verses carry no readable verse number in the source edition. Their text is present and their chapters hold the expected number of verses; only the printed number could not be read. Each is placed by the range it falls inside. See discrepancies.json.",
  "verse_division_differences": {
   "chapters": [
    "Exodus 20",
    "Deuteronomy 5"
   ],
   "note": "The two Decalogue passages are divided into verses differently by different editions, a long-standing variation tied to the two cantillation traditions for the Ten Commandments. This edition gives Exodus 20 twenty-two verses where Sefaria gives twenty-three, and Deuteronomy 5 twenty-nine where Sefaria gives thirty. A reference taken from another edition may therefore sit one verse away from the same words here. It cannot lead to the wrong unit: each of those two chapters lies wholly inside a single unit — Exodus 20 in Exodus unit 7, Deuteronomy 5 in Deuteronomy unit 3 — so any verse of either chapter resolves to that unit under either numbering."
  }
 },
